// Numerals: what the program writes, the program can read back
//
// Rendering a number in a script is only half of it. Every numeric cast accepts
// digits from any of the 69 supported scripts, so a value rendered under an
// active mode parses back exactly like its ASCII twin: `#|…|`, `#.N|…|` and
// `#!N|…|` all normalize before parsing.
//
// A string that is not a number at all is still rejected — that behaviour is
// unchanged, and identical in both engines.

>> "--- literals in a non-ASCII script, ASCII mode ---" ¶
>> #|"४२"| ¶
>> #.1|"४२"| ¶
>> #!1|"४२.७८"| ¶
>> #|"-४२"| ¶
>> #|"३.५"| ¶

>> "--- ASCII digits still behave the same ---" ¶
>> #|"42"| ¶
>> #.1|"42"| ¶
>> #!1|"42.78"| ¶

#०९#

>> "--- the full round trip under an active mode ---" ¶
n = 120
s = "{n}"
>> "rendered      " s ¶
>> "read back     " #|s| ¶
>> "rounded       " #.0|s| ¶

// The value that came back is a number, not text: arithmetic works on it.
back = #|s|
>> "plus one      " (back + 1) ¶

#09#

>> "--- and in ASCII it is the same 120 ---" ¶
>> back ¶
>> (back * 2) ¶
